  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` couldn't handle prices with a thousands separator, such as `\"2,450.50\"`. Its regex only allowed digits and an optional decimal part, so `match()` returned `null`. The next line then read `m[1]` and threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`, which `/checkout` turned into a 500.\n\nIn `logs/app.log` there are 37 `/checkout` 500s. Every failing payload had a comma-grouped price (`1,299.00`, `2,450.50` or `1,005.99`). No other price format in the log failed.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- The regex now accepts comma-grouped thousands, so `2,450.50` parses to 245050 cents.\n- Input that still isn't a valid amount now throws `Error('Invalid amount: <input>')`, as the function's own comment promises. Before, it crashed with a `TypeError`.\n- Malformed input will still return a 500, but with a clear error message. I didn't change the server to return a 4xx for bad prices.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- Parsing of the three prices from the log, plus `1,000,000`.\n- Malformed amounts (`abc`, empty string, `1,29.00`, `12.345`, `-5`) throw \"Invalid amount\".\n- A checkout with a comma-formatted price (`1,299.00` × 2 gives 259800).\n\n`npm test` passes with 7 tests. I didn't run the first two new tests against the old code. The checkout test would have failed on it, because that is the path that produced the 500s.",
